<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-diagonalization" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Multiplicity, Eigenspaces, and Diagonalization</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-multiplicity">
    <title>Multiplicity and Basic Eigenvectors</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-basic-eigenvector">
      <title>Basic Eigenvector</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A <term>basic eigenvector</term> of an <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> is any
          nonzero multiple of a basic solution to <m>(\lambda I - A)\vec{x} = 0</m>, where
          <m>\lambda</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-repeated-eigenvalue">
      <title>An Eigenvalue of Multiplicity Two</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 3 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \det(\lambda I - A)
              \amp = \det \begin{bmatrix}
                \lambda - 4 \amp -1 \amp -2 \\
                0 \amp \lambda - 3 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp \lambda - 2
              \end{bmatrix}
              = (\lambda - 4) \begin{vmatrix} \lambda - 3 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp \lambda - 2 \end{vmatrix}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = (\lambda - 4) \left[ (\lambda - 3)(\lambda - 2) - 2 \right]
              = (\lambda - 4)\left( \lambda^2 - 5\lambda + 4 \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = (\lambda - 4)^2 (\lambda - 1).
            </mrow>
          </md>
          We say that <m>\lambda = 4</m> has <term>multiplicity</term> <m>2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>For <m>\lambda_1 = 4</m>,</term> solving <m>(4I - A)\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>:
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              0 \amp -1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 0
            \end{array} \right]
            \longrightarrow
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{array} \right]
            \; \Longrightarrow \;
            \vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} s \\ -2t \\ t \end{bmatrix}
            = s \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
            + t \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Hence the eigenvalue <m>\lambda_1 = 4</m> has two corresponding basic
          eigenvectors,
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Note that the basic
          eigenvectors are NOT scalar multiples of each other.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>For <m>\lambda_2 = 1</m>:</term>
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              -3 \amp -1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -2 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0
            \end{array} \right]
            \longrightarrow
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              -3 \amp -1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{array} \right]
            \; \Longrightarrow \;
            \vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} -t \\ t \\ t \end{bmatrix}
            = t \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          In summary, <m>A</m> has two eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1 = 4</m> and
          <m>\lambda_2 = 1</m> with multiplicities <m>2</m> and <m>1</m> respectively.
          There are two basic eigenvectors corresponding to <m>\lambda_1 = 4</m> and one
          basic eigenvector corresponding to <m>\lambda_2 = 1</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Note that any linear combination of basic eigenvectors corresponding to the same
          eigenvalue is also an eigenvector of <m>A</m>. For instance, since
          <md>
            A \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} = 4 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
            \qquad \text{and} \qquad
            A \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = 4 \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          it follows that
          <md>
            A \left( 2 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + 3 \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right)
            = 4 \left( 2 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + 3 \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right).
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-three-distinct-eigenvalues">
      <title>Three Distinct Eigenvalues</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of
          <m>B = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp -4 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp -2 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp -5 \amp 5 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Expanding <m>\det(\lambda I - B)</m> and factoring gives
          <md>
            \det(\lambda I - B) = (\lambda - 3)(\lambda^2 - 3\lambda + 2)
            = (\lambda - 3)(\lambda - 1)(\lambda - 2),
          </md>
          so <m>\lambda_1 = 1</m>, <m>\lambda_2 = 2</m>, <m>\lambda_3 = 3</m>. They all
          have multiplicity one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Finding the eigenvectors.</term>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \lambda_1 = 1: \amp \quad (I - B)\vec{x} = \vec{0}
              \; \longrightarrow \;
              \vec{x} = t \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \lambda_2 = 2: \amp \quad (2I - B)\vec{x} = \vec{0}
              \; \longrightarrow \;
              \vec{x} = t \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \lambda_3 = 3: \amp \quad (3I - B)\vec{x} = \vec{0}
              \; \longrightarrow \;
              \vec{x} = \tfrac{t}{2} \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}.
            </mrow>
          </md>
          So
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m> are basic eigenvectors
          corresponding to <m>\lambda_1 = 1</m>, <m>\lambda_2 = 2</m>, and
          <m>\lambda_3 = 3</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-eigenspace">
    <title>Eigenspaces</title>

    <p>
      Note that if an <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> has <m>m</m> distinct basic
      eigenvectors <m>\vec{x}_1, \vec{x}_2, \ldots, \vec{x}_m</m>, then, as we have seen
      in the previous examples, the set
      <m>\{ \vec{x}_1, \vec{x}_2, \ldots, \vec{x}_m \}</m> is a linearly independent set
      (this follows from the definition of a basic eigenvector). Hence
      <m>\operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{x}_1, \ldots, \vec{x}_m \}</m> is a subspace.
    </p>
    <p>
      In fact, since the vectors <m>\vec{x}_i</m> (<m>1 \leq i \leq m</m>) are solutions
      of <m>(\lambda I - A)\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>, we know that
      <md>
        \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{x}_1, \vec{x}_2, \ldots, \vec{x}_m \}
        = \operatorname{Nul}(\lambda I - A).
      </md>
      Since this set is a subspace of <m>\R^n</m>, it is known as the
      <term>eigenspace</term> of <m>A</m>.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-diagonalizable-iff">
      <title>Diagonalizability</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n \times n</m> matrix. Then <m>A</m> is diagonalizable if
          and only if the matrix <m>A</m> has <m>n</m> linearly independent eigenvectors.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Assume that <m>A</m> has <m>n</m> linearly independent eigenvectors
          <m>\vec{x}_1, \vec{x}_2, \ldots, \vec{x}_n</m>. Then the matrix
          <m>P = \begin{bmatrix} \vec{x}_1 \amp \vec{x}_2 \amp \cdots \amp \vec{x}_n \end{bmatrix}</m>
          is invertible and we have
          <md>
            <mrow>
              P^{-1}AP
              \amp = P^{-1} \begin{bmatrix} A\vec{x}_1 \amp A\vec{x}_2 \amp \cdots \amp A\vec{x}_n \end{bmatrix}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = P^{-1} \begin{bmatrix} \lambda_1\vec{x}_1 \amp \lambda_2\vec{x}_2 \amp \cdots \amp \lambda_n\vec{x}_n \end{bmatrix}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = P^{-1} \begin{bmatrix} \vec{x}_1 \amp \vec{x}_2 \amp \cdots \amp \vec{x}_n \end{bmatrix}
                \begin{bmatrix}
                  \lambda_1 \amp \amp \amp \\
                  \amp \lambda_2 \amp \amp \\
                  \amp \amp \ddots \amp \\
                  \amp \amp \amp \lambda_n
                \end{bmatrix}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = P^{-1}PD = D,
            </mrow>
          </md>
          and hence <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.
        </p>
        <p>
          Conversely, if <m>A</m> is diagonalizable, then there exists an invertible
          matrix <m>P</m> such that <m>D = P^{-1}AP</m> is diagonal, hence
          <m>AP = PD</m>. Now assume
          <m>\vec{x}_1, \vec{x}_2, \ldots, \vec{x}_n</m> are columns of <m>P</m>. Then
          comparing columns of <m>AP = PD</m> gives, for <m>i = 1, 2, \ldots, n</m>,
          <md>
            A\vec{x}_i = \lambda_i \vec{x}_i,
          </md>
          which means the diagonal entries of <m>D</m> are the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> and
          the <m>\vec{x}_i</m> are their corresponding basic eigenvectors, which are
          linearly independent since we know <m>P</m> is invertible.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <remark xml:id="rem-revisit-examples">
      <p>
        Revisiting the previous examples: note that <m>A</m> in
        <xref ref="eg-repeated-eigenvalue" text="type-global" /> is <m>3 \times 3</m> and
        has <m>3</m> distinct basic eigenvectors. As a result, <m>A</m> is
        diagonalizable. Also, notice that <m>B</m> in
        <xref ref="eg-three-distinct-eigenvalues" text="type-global" /> has <m>3</m>
        distinct eigenvalues and each eigenvalue has a basic eigenvector corresponding to
        it, hence <m>B</m> is diagonalizable as well.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-characterizing-diagonalizable">
      <title>Characterizing Diagonalizable Matrices</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A square matrix <m>A</m> is diagonalizable if and only if every eigenvalue
          <m>\lambda</m> of multiplicity <m>m</m> yields exactly <m>m</m> basic
          eigenvectors, i.e., the solution to <m>(\lambda I - A)X = 0</m> has <m>m</m>
          parameters.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <remark xml:id="rem-distinct-eigenvalues-diagonalizable">
      <p>
        A special case of this result is when every eigenvalue of a matrix has
        multiplicity one. An <m>n \times n</m> matrix with <m>n</m> distinct eigenvalues
        is diagonalizable.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-similar-matrices">
    <title>Similar Matrices</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-similar">
      <title>Similar Matrices</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be <m>n \times n</m> matrices and suppose there exists
          an invertible matrix <m>P</m> such that <m>A = P^{-1}BP</m>. Then <m>A</m> and
          <m>B</m> are called <term>similar matrices</term>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-similar-matrices">
      <title>Two Similar Matrices</title>
      <p>
        Let
        <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 4 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
        <m>B = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp 4 \\ 1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
        <m>P = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>, so that
        <m>P^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Then
        <md>
          P^{-1}BP
          = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp 4 \\ 1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 3 \\ 2 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 4 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix} = A,
        </md>
        so <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are similar matrices.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Observation.</term> Here <m>P</m> is obtained from <m>I</m> by switching the
        columns, and right-multiplying <m>B</m> by <m>P</m> switches the columns of
        <m>B</m>.
      </p>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-similar-same-eigenvalues">
      <title>Similar Matrices Have the Same Eigenvalues</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be similar matrices such that <m>A = P^{-1}BP</m>,
          where <m>A</m>, <m>B</m> are <m>n \times n</m> matrices and <m>P</m> is
          invertible. Then <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> have the same eigenvalues.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \det(\lambda I - A)
              \amp = \det \left( \lambda I - P^{-1}BP \right)
              = \det \left( P^{-1}\lambda I P - P^{-1}BP \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \det \left( P^{-1}(\lambda I - B)P \right)
              = \det(P^{-1}) \det(\lambda I - B) \det(P)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \frac{1}{\det(P)} \det(\lambda I - B) \det(P)
              = \det(\lambda I - B),
            </mrow>
          </md>
          using <m>P^{-1}P = I</m> in the first line. Hence <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> have the
          same characteristic polynomial and therefore the same eigenvalues.
        </p>
        <p>
          In particular, if <m>A</m> is diagonalizable, then <m>A</m> is similar to a
          diagonal matrix <m>D</m>, and hence the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> and <m>D</m> are
          the same, which means the diagonal entries of <m>D</m> are the eigenvalues of
          <m>A</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <example xml:id="eg-similar-shortcut">
      <title>Using Similarity to Find Eigenvalues</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the previous theorem to find the eigenvalues of the following matrix:
          <md>
            A = \begin{bmatrix}
              33 \amp 105 \amp 105 \\
              10 \amp 28 \amp 30 \\
              -20 \amp -60 \amp -62
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Note that a row operation followed by a column operation produces a much simpler
          matrix. Applying <m>2R_2 + R_3</m> (left-multiplying by <m>E</m>) and then
          <m>-2C_3 + C_2</m> (right-multiplying by <m>E^{-1}</m>):
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix}
              33 \amp 105 \amp 105 \\ 10 \amp 28 \amp 30 \\ -20 \amp -60 \amp -62
            \end{bmatrix}
            \xrightarrow{2R_2 + R_3}
            \begin{bmatrix}
              33 \amp 105 \amp 105 \\ 10 \amp 28 \amp 30 \\ 0 \amp -4 \amp -2
            \end{bmatrix}
            \xrightarrow{-2C_3 + C_2}
            B = \begin{bmatrix}
              33 \amp -105 \amp 105 \\ 10 \amp -32 \amp 30 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp -2
            \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          where
          <md>
            E = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            E^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -2 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Thus <m>B = EAE^{-1}</m>, or <m>A = E^{-1}BE</m>, so <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are
          similar matrices and have the same eigenvalues. Since <m>B</m> has a row of
          zeros in its lower-left corner, its characteristic polynomial factors
          immediately:
          <md>
            \det(\lambda I - A) = \det(\lambda I - B)
            = -(-2 - \lambda) \begin{vmatrix} 33 - \lambda \amp -105 \\ 10 \amp -32 - \lambda \end{vmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-imt-eigenvalue">
      <title>Another Addition to the Invertible Matrix Theorem</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n \times n</m> matrix. Then <m>A</m> is invertible if and
          only if <m>0</m> is not one of its eigenvalues.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          We know that <m>A</m> is invertible if and only if <m>\det A \neq 0</m>. To
          prove the above theorem, we need to show that <m>\det A \neq 0</m> if and only
          if <m>0</m> is not one of its eigenvalues.
        </p>
        <p>
          We first show that <m>\det A = \lambda_1 \lambda_2 \cdots \lambda_n</m>, where
          the <m>\lambda_i</m> are the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>. Remember that the
          <m>\lambda_i</m> are roots of the characteristic polynomial, hence
          <md>
            \det(\lambda I - A) = (\lambda - \lambda_1)(\lambda - \lambda_2) \cdots (\lambda - \lambda_n).
          </md>
          Now choose <m>\lambda = 0</m>. Then
          <md>
            \det(-A) = (-1)^n \lambda_1 \lambda_2 \cdots \lambda_n.
          </md>
          Also, we know <m>\det(-A) = (-1)^n \det A</m>. Therefore
          <md>
            \det A = \lambda_1 \lambda_2 \cdots \lambda_n.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally, it is clear that if one of the eigenvalues is zero, then
          <m>\det A = 0</m>, and if <m>\det A = 0</m> then at least one of the eigenvalues
          must be zero.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-counterexample-diagonalization">
    <title>Challenge: Build a Counterexample</title>

    <p xml:id="challenge-positive-eigenvalues">
      Consider the claim: <q>Every real matrix whose eigenvalues are all
      positive is diagonalizable over <m>\R</m>.</q> Construct a
      <m>3\times3</m> counterexample <m>A</m> satisfying all of these conditions:
    </p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>Every entry of <m>A</m> is a nonzero integer.</p></li>
        <li>
          <p>Its characteristic polynomial is
            <md>\det(tI-A)=(t-1)^2(t-2)=t^3-4t^2+5t-2.</md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li><p>The eigenspace for <m>\lambda=1</m> has dimension one.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p>
      Verify your construction by computing the characteristic polynomial,
      finding a basis for each eigenspace, and explaining why <m>A</m> is
      invertible but not diagonalizable. Use
      <xref ref="thm-diagonalizable-iff" text="type-global" /> and
      <xref ref="thm-imt-eigenvalue" text="type-global" /> to justify your conclusion.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-counterexample-diagonalization">
      <caption>
        Build and check your matrix. After it satisfies all three conditions,
        use Show verification to compare the eigenspace bases with your own work.
        Different valid matrices are accepted.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="int-counterexample-diagonalization"
        iframe="demos/counterexample-diagonalization.html" width="100%" aspect="6:7">
        <shortdescription>
          A three-by-three integer matrix builder checks the characteristic
          polynomial and eigenspace dimension, then verifies a counterexample
          to the claim that positive eigenvalues guarantee diagonalizability.
        </shortdescription>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
    <p>
      <url href="external/demos/counterexample-diagonalization.html">Open the matrix builder on its own.</url>
      For written work, start with nine unknown entries and impose the three
      conditions above; the problem can also be completed without the builder.
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-predict-transient-growth">
    <title>Challenge: Predict, Then Animate</title>
    <p>
      Let
      <md xml:id="eq-predict-growth-matrix">
        A_\varepsilon=\begin{bmatrix}
          \tfrac23\amp6\\
          0\amp\tfrac23-\varepsilon
        \end{bmatrix},\qquad 0\le\varepsilon\le\tfrac13,
      </md>
      and define
      <md xml:id="eq-predict-growth-recurrence">
        \vec{x}_{n+1}=A_\varepsilon\vec{x}_n,\qquad
        \vec{x}_0=\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix}.
      </md>
      Measure the size of the vector by
      <md xml:id="eq-predict-growth-size">
        M_n=\max\{|x_{n,1}|,|x_{n,2}|\}.
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Before playing the animation, record predictions and justify them:
      <ol>
        <li><p>For which values of <m>\varepsilon</m> is <m>A_\varepsilon</m> diagonalizable?</p></li>
        <li><p>Does <m>\vec{x}_n</m> approach the origin for every parameter value? Can <m>M_n</m> increase initially?</p></li>
        <li><p>What is the exact maximum of <m>M_n</m>, and which iteration or iterations attain it? Include both parameter endpoints.</p></li>
        <li><p>As <m>\varepsilon</m> approaches zero, the eigenvector directions approach one another. Does this force the maximum excursion to become unbounded?</p></li>
      </ol>
      Use diagonalization where it applies and a direct calculation in any
      exceptional case. Compare your predictions with the trajectory and the
      graph of <m>M_n</m> before opening the explanation.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-predict-transient-growth">
      <caption>
        Record all four predictions to unlock playback. The vector trajectory,
        eigendirections, and size graph share the same parameter and iteration.
        Pause, step through the iterates, and compare the parameter endpoints.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="int-predict-transient-growth"
        iframe="demos/predict-transient-growth.html" width="100%" aspect="6:9">
        <shortdescription>
          A prediction-gated animation of a two-dimensional matrix recurrence.
          Synchronized plots show the vector orbit, eigendirections, and the
          maximum absolute coordinate as the parameter and iteration change.
        </shortdescription>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
    <p>
      <url href="external/demos/predict-transient-growth.html">Open the prediction challenge on its own.</url>
      The matrix, recurrence, and questions above also give a complete written
      version of the challenge.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
